  "live_records": {
    "q-33681": {
      "dom_id": "step-q33681",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-6t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}(-2\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}[2t\\,i+(2-{t}^{2})\\,j+2t\\,k].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=1$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}(-2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+(1)^{2}\\,k)=\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k) \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}[2(1)\\,i+(2-(1)^{2})\\,j+2(1)\\,k]=\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=1$ is\n\n$$\np=r(1)=-6(1)\\,i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+(1)^{3}\\,k=⟨-6,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k))\\times (\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k]) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(3\\,i+6\\,j-6\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=3\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i+2\\,j-2\\,k=⟨1,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ & =⟨-6,3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+2\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-2 \\\\ x+2y-2z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
