    "q-127321": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127321",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-1⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
